            <ArticleTitle>Anomalous course of the intracranial internal carotid artery into the orbit: case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1136/neurintsurg-2011-010037</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In this report, a case of anomalous internal carotid artery looping into the orbital apex is presented. The patient was a 41-year-old man with sudden onset headache, suggestive of aneurysmal subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH). Imaging with CT demonstrated a perimesencephalic distribution of blood. Cerebral angiography confirmed non-aneurysmal, perimesencephalic SAH, but incidentally noted an anomalous left internal carotid artery with a course into the orbital cone. This is the only known example of this anatomic variation. Potential embryological explanations are discussed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
